### What problem does structured concurrency solve?

Unstructured concurrency, like submitting tasks to an executor and holding futures, has no enforced relationship between the parent and its subtasks. If the parent returns early or throws, the subtasks keep running as orphans, consuming resources and logging confusing errors. Cancellation is manual and easy to get wrong.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class UnstructuredProblem {

    private final ExecutorService pool =
            Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);

    // PROBLEM: if creditCheck fails, fraudCheck keeps running
    // as an orphan, wasting resources
    public boolean approveTransfer(String userId) throws Exception {
        Future<Boolean> creditFuture =
                pool.submit(() -> checkCredit(userId));
        Future<Boolean> fraudFuture =
                pool.submit(() -> checkFraud(userId));

        try {
            boolean credit = creditFuture.get(); // blocks
            boolean fraud = fraudFuture.get();   // blocks
            return credit && fraud;
        } catch (ExecutionException e) {
            // Must manually cancel the other future!
            creditFuture.cancel(true);
            fraudFuture.cancel(true);
            throw e;
        }
    }

    private boolean checkCredit(String userId) {
        return true;
    }

    private boolean checkFraud(String userId) {
        return true;
    }
}
```

### How does StructuredTaskScope work?

You open a scope in try-with-resources, fork each subtask, call join to wait, and read results. The scope's close guarantees every subtask has finished before the parent returns.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.StructuredTaskScope;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class StructuredTransferService {

    public boolean approveTransfer(String userId) throws Exception {
        try (var scope = new StructuredTaskScope
                .ShutdownOnFailure()) {

            // Fork subtasks: each runs on a virtual thread
            var creditTask = scope.fork(
                    () -> checkCredit(userId));
            var fraudTask = scope.fork(
                    () -> checkFraud(userId));
            var incomeTask = scope.fork(
                    () -> verifyIncome(userId));

            // Wait for all to complete or first failure
            scope.join();
            scope.throwIfFailed();

            // All succeeded: read results
            boolean credit = creditTask.get();
            boolean fraud = fraudTask.get();
            boolean income = incomeTask.get();

            return credit && fraud && income;
        }
        // close() guarantees all subtasks are done
        // No orphans possible
    }

    private boolean checkCredit(String userId) {
        System.out.println("Credit check for " + userId);
        return true;
    }

    private boolean checkFraud(String userId) {
        System.out.println("Fraud check for " + userId);
        return true;
    }

    private boolean verifyIncome(String userId) {
        System.out.println("Income verify for " + userId);
        return true;
    }
}
```

### How does ShutdownOnFailure work?

With a ShutdownOnFailure policy, the first subtask that throws triggers cancellation of all other still-running subtasks. After join, throwIfFailed rethrows the first exception. This is the pattern when several independent checks must all pass.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.StructuredTaskScope;

public class FailFastDemo {

    public boolean approveTransfer(String userId) throws Exception {
        try (var scope = new StructuredTaskScope
                .ShutdownOnFailure()) {

            var creditTask = scope.fork(
                    () -> checkCredit(userId));
            var fraudTask = scope.fork(
                    () -> checkFraud(userId));

            scope.join();

            // If fraud check threw, credit check was cancelled
            scope.throwIfFailed();

            return creditTask.get() && fraudTask.get();
        }
    }

    private boolean checkCredit(String userId) {
        try { Thread.sleep(2000); } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
        return true;
    }

    private boolean checkFraud(String userId) {
        // Fails fast: other tasks get cancelled
        throw new RuntimeException("Fraud detected for " + userId);
    }
}
```

### How does ShutdownOnSuccess work?

With ShutdownOnSuccess, the first subtask that succeeds triggers cancellation of the rest. This is the pattern for racing several equivalent sources and taking the fastest.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.StructuredTaskScope;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class RaceForRateService {

    // Race multiple providers for the best exchange rate
    public BigDecimal getExchangeRate(String currency)
            throws Exception {
        try (var scope = new StructuredTaskScope
                .ShutdownOnSuccess<BigDecimal>()) {

            scope.fork(() -> fetchFromProviderA(currency));
            scope.fork(() -> fetchFromProviderB(currency));
            scope.fork(() -> fetchFromProviderC(currency));

            scope.join();

            // First successful result wins;
            // other providers cancelled
            return scope.result();
        }
    }

    private BigDecimal fetchFromProviderA(String currency) {
        try { Thread.sleep(100); } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
        return new BigDecimal("83.50");
    }

    private BigDecimal fetchFromProviderB(String currency) {
        try { Thread.sleep(200); } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
        return new BigDecimal("83.52");
    }

    private BigDecimal fetchFromProviderC(String currency) {
        try { Thread.sleep(300); } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
        return new BigDecimal("83.48");
    }
}
```

### How is structured concurrency different from executor-based concurrency? (Comparison)

| Feature | StructuredTaskScope | ExecutorService + Future |
|---|---|---|
| Orphan prevention | Guaranteed by close() | Manual cancel on every path |
| Cancellation | Automatic via policy | Manual future.cancel() |
| Fail-fast | ShutdownOnFailure | Custom logic per future |
| Race pattern | ShutdownOnSuccess | Custom CompletableFuture |
| Thread type | Virtual threads (cheap) | Platform threads (heavy) |
| Scoped values | Inherited cheaply | InheritableThreadLocal (expensive) |
| Deadline support | join(Duration) | Custom timeout logic |
| Code clarity | Linear, structured | Scattered, callback-heavy |

### How does structured concurrency compose with deadlines?

You can join with a timeout so the whole group is bounded in time. If the deadline expires, remaining subtasks are cancelled.

```java
import java.time.Duration;

try (var scope = new StructuredTaskScope.ShutdownOnFailure()) {
    var creditTask = scope.fork(() -> checkCredit("Rajesh"));
    var fraudTask = scope.fork(() -> checkFraud("Rajesh"));

    // Entire group must complete within 5 seconds
    scope.joinUntil(java.time.Instant.now().plusSeconds(5));
    scope.throwIfFailed();

    return creditTask.get() && fraudTask.get();
}
```

### How does it compose with scoped values?

Child tasks forked from a StructuredTaskScope transparently inherit scoped-value bindings cheaply, giving context propagation for free without copying a per-thread map.

```java
import java.lang.ScopedValue;

static final ScopedValue<String> CURRENT_USER =
        ScopedValue.newInstance();

ScopedValue.where(CURRENT_USER, "Rajesh").run(() -> {
    try (var scope = new StructuredTaskScope.ShutdownOnFailure()) {
        // Both children inherit CURRENT_USER = "Rajesh"
        scope.fork(() -> {
            System.out.println(CURRENT_USER.get()); // Rajesh
            return checkCredit(CURRENT_USER.get());
        });
        scope.fork(() -> {
            System.out.println(CURRENT_USER.get()); // Rajesh
            return checkFraud(CURRENT_USER.get());
        });
        scope.join();
        scope.throwIfFailed();
    }
});
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Structured concurrency makes concurrent code read like ordinary structured code with clear error handling instead of a tangle of futures. The single guarantee that close prevents orphans is the key design insight. The newest API iterations fold the policy into how you open the scope and provide a joiner abstraction, but the core principles of fork into a bounded scope, join, and guaranteed shutdown are stable.
